  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Who narrates Wuthering Heights?",
      "a": "Two narrators: Lockwood, a tenant at Thrushcross Grange who writes the frame, and Nelly Dean, the housekeeper who tells him most of the history. Other voices reach us through them, such as Isabella's letter.",
      "asks": [
        "Who tells the story in Wuthering Heights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does Heathcliff want revenge?",
      "a": "After Mr Earnshaw dies, Hindley treats him as a servant, and he believes Catherine has rejected him for Edgar Linton. He spends the rest of his life taking both families' property and happiness.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is Heathcliff so cruel?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does I am Heathcliff mean?",
      "a": "Catherine tells Nelly she and Heathcliff are made of the same soul, so loving him is like being herself. Yet she still marries Edgar, which splits her in two.",
      "asks": [
        "Nelly, I am Heathcliff meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does Wuthering Heights end?",
      "a": "Heathcliff loses his will for revenge, stops eating and dies longing for Catherine. Young Cathy and Hareton plan to marry, and local people say the ghosts of Heathcliff and Catherine walk the moors.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens to Heathcliff at the end?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is Heathcliff a hero or a villain?",
      "a": "Both readings exist. He is an abused outsider and a passionate lover, but also a violent abuser of Isabella, Hareton and young Cathy. Some critics, such as Terry Eagleton, read his rise through class and property.",
      "asks": [
        "Is Heathcliff a Byronic hero?"
      ]
    }
  ],
